A thriving local economy does not happen by accident. It requires capital in the right hands, markets that work for everyone, and a workforce trained for the jobs of today and tomorrow. Bill Llia's economic agenda delivers all three.

**Small business and cooperative support:** We will establish the Nairobi County Enterprise Fund — an initial allocation of KES 500 million — to provide affordable credit to small businesses and cooperatives at single-digit interest rates. We will cut business permit processing time from weeks to 48 hours and digitize the entire system to end corruption. Monthly market days in every ward will connect local producers directly with buyers, keeping money circulating within our communities.

**Farmer empowerment:** Nairobi County's farmers lose up to 40% of their produce to spoilage because they lack cold storage and reliable transport. Bill will invest in aggregation centers with cold storage facilities in each constituency, negotiate bulk purchase agreements with Nairobi's hotels and schools, and ensure every farming cooperative has access to quality seeds, fertilizers, and extension services. When farmers earn more, every table in Nairobi County has more food.

**Youth and women enterprise funds:** A dedicated KES 200 million fund will provide startup grants of up to KES 100,000 for young entrepreneurs and women-led businesses, paired with mentorship from established business leaders. We will partner with technical and vocational institutions to create apprenticeship placements that lead directly to permanent employment, with a target of 10,000 young people placed within two years.

This is not a wish list. It is a funded, phased, accountable plan to turn Nairobi County's economy around — starting on day one.
